{"reference":"1 Corinthians 11","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":11,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"language_literary","title":"Head, appearance, and mutual dependence","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"In verse 3, the Greek word kephale means “head,” but its relational force is disputed: interpreters have proposed source, authority, prominence, or a combination. The passage also joins covered and uncovered heads, hair, shame, glory, nature, and custom without preserving a clear material description of the practice in question. These uncertainties mean the passage should not be treated as self-evident proof of one timeless gender hierarchy.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 11:3","1 Corinthians 11:4-16","1 Corinthians 11:5"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_011_5a6de703be68930609099cb7"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"The passage regulates how both women and men pray and prophesy; it does not portray women as absent from this speech. Verses 11–12 then insist that woman and man are not independent of one another in the Lord, pairing woman’s origin from man with every man’s birth through woman. Any reading of the distinctions in this paragraph needs to account for that reciprocal dependence as well.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 11:2-16","1 Corinthians 11:8-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_011_b715d4bed8e8d8d199cfe17d"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"The meal and unequal access","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Paul’s account of the bread and cup is embedded in a rebuke about the actual meal. Some proceed with their own supper, so that one person remains hungry while another becomes intoxicated; Paul therefore says their gathering is not truly the Lord’s Supper. The inherited tradition of remembrance and proclamation is not detached from this failure of shared participation.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 11:20-21","1 Corinthians 11:23-26","1 Corinthians 11:29-32"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_011_c635fd1e37848ec80dc8843f"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_4","text":"Comparisons with ancient associations and banquets help explain why arrival time, food quantity, honor, and public humiliation could shape a communal meal. They do not prove a particular meeting-room arrangement. The wider social setting included people with unequal resources and vulnerability, making Paul’s concern for those left hungry especially concrete.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 11:17-34"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_011_b879a68a280fa0c26672c124"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Warning, self-examination, and silence","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"“In an unworthy manner” describes the manner of eating and drinking, rather than creating a category of people who must first become perfectly worthy. Paul’s practical remedy is coordinated participation: receive one another, and address private hunger at home. Although the passage links judgment with weakness, sickness, and death, it does not authorize treating illness, disability, or death as proof of sacramental failure or divine punishment.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 11:27-29","1 Corinthians 11:29-32","1 Corinthians 11:33-34"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_011_397cdedcbd14b18608355e63"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_6","text":"“Discerning the body” is debated. It may refer to Messiah’s body, the gathered body of believers, the meal itself, or overlapping senses. The surrounding concern with bread, cup, humiliation, sickness, and waiting means that doctrinal attention and regard for fellow participants should not be set against each other.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 11:17-34","1 Corinthians 11:20-22","1 Corinthians 11:27","1 Corinthians 11:29","1 Corinthians 11:29-32"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_011_eaeb0220287a270f8543669e"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_7","text":"Chapter 11’s assumption that women pray and prophesy matters when reading later difficult silence language in the same letter. The same verb for silence is also used for an uninterpreted tongue speaker and for a prophet yielding the floor when another receives revelation. The scope of those instructions remains disputed, so they should not be converted into a total or permanent speech ban.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 11:5"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_011_fc6f6101119b7a6a29aa2076"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Reading the table’s concern carefully","blocks":[{"id":"block_8","text":"Questions involving allergy, disability, addiction, eating disorders, poverty, food safety, or interfaith families are modern questions, not hidden first-century diagnoses in this chapter. Its stated concerns—hunger, dignity, waiting, shared participation, welcome, and avoiding harm—can guide reflection on such questions without pretending that Paul directly names them.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 11:17-34"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_011_ad070dbc5f9a70ea086f3d21"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Nearby meal discussions","blocks":[{"id":"block_9","text":"The surrounding argument distinguishes several kinds of meal setting: disputed household practice, cultic dining, market food, private hospitality, the assembly’s supper, and table withdrawal. These distinctions caution against treating every discussion of food, conscience, or shared tables as though it were the same case.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 10:1-33","1 Corinthians 8:1-13","1 Corinthians 9:1-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_011_49d9c5fecd0991ef4ef98cc7"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}